lesson.text:
You built this in the first lesson. The gap is the central venous PCO2 minus the arterial PCO2. Normal is two to six, and above six means hypoperfusion.

{{pic}}

Remember the dye in the river. The tissues pour CO2 into the blood at a steady rate, and the flow carries it away. By the Fick principle, the gap is roughly the CO2 production divided by the cardiac output. So when the output falls, the CO2 piles up in the venous blood. It marks flow, not hypoxia. And it reveals low flow even when the lactate is normal.
